  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 317 AM CDT Mon Aug 17 2026\n\n(Tuesday Night - Thursday)\n\nThe coverage of showers and thunderstorms should increase across the \narea quickly Tuesday night as the nocturnal low-level jet \nstrengthens and a fairly potent midlevel shortwave trough approaches \nfrom the northwest. Storms look pretty progressive along/north of a \nweak cold frontal passage, which should limit rainfall totals. There \nis a conditional threat of a few strong to severe thunderstorms, but \nthere is low confidence that the area will be unstable enough to \nsustain severe convection. Moisture return ahead of this boundary is \nthe big question mark, and therefore, instability. Recent trends \nhave been more toward limited moisture return, with SBCAPE \nprobabilities of at least 1000 J/kg late Tuesday night from the \nGEFS/GEPS dropping into the ~10% range. The machine-learning CSU \nguidance also has trended downward with its severe probabilities \nacross the region Tuesday night. However, individual deterministic \nmodels (e.g., NAM/ECMWF) show at least the potential for higher \ninstability, so this will be a period we will need to watch over the \nnext 12-24+ hours. What is not a question mark is the anomalously \nstrong deep-layer shear, which should be in the 35-45+ knot range. \nTherefore, if there is sufficient instability, thunderstorms would \nlikely organize into a line capable of producing damaging wind \ngusts. \n\nAnother (slower) moving midlevel shortwave trough is forecast to \ncross the Mississippi Valley between Wednesday night and Thursday. \nThis feature should also help yield another round of showers and \nthunderstorms, with the best chances (50-70%) along/south of I-70 \nnear the 850-hPa frontal zone. This frontal zone is nearly quasi-\nstationary, only oozing southward with time. Given the potential for \na long(er) duration of forcing for ascent and the potential for \nshowers and thunderstorms, there may be a low threat for heavy \nrainfall and flash flooding across the eastern Ozarks during this \ntime period. \n\n\n(Thursday Night - Sunday)\n\nA surface ridge is likely to move into the mid-Mississippi Valley to \nend the work week heading into the weekend. Near to below normal \ntemperatures and mostly dry weather is forecast Thursday night \nthrough Saturday. Ensemble guidance shows a broad mid/upper level \ntrough digging into the Great Lakes/northeast by Sunday, though \nthere are differences with the strength of the trough. Two of the \nfour clusters of the 500-hPa pattern on the LREF are pretty sharp \nwith this feature, which would likely bring a shot of much cooler \ntemperatures into the region. These two clusters comprise about half \nof the total LREF members. Meanwhile, the other half shows a much \nweaker trough. What is interesting is that a pretty large majority \nof both the EPS/GEPS favor the stronger trough and cooler air mass \nmaking into our region while the GEFS has an even larger percentage \nin the weaker trough/cold front scenario. Most typically, when 1 of \nthe 3 ensemble suites does not match the other two, it is not \ncorrect. I would therefore at least slightly favor the cooler \ntemperature scenario to end the weekend heading into early the \nfollowing week.",
